<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <title>Fire Intelligence & Soil Risk Model | {siteConfig.name}</title>
  </head>
  <body class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 min-h-screen flex flex-col font-sans">
    <Header />

    <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 flex-grow">
      <!-- Navigation Back Link -->
      <a href="/portfolio/" class="text-emerald-400 hover:text-emerald-300 font-medium mb-6 inline-block">
        ← Back to Portfolio
      </a>

      <!-- Page Title & Overview -->
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-4 text-white">
        Full-Cycle Fire Intelligence & Soil Risk Model
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-emerald-400 font-semibold mb-3">
        Evaluating Prescribed Burn Severity, Structural Efficacy, and Watershed Risks in Google Earth Engine
      </p>

      <p class="text-lg text-gray-300 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
        Engineered a 33-band cloud spatial pipeline in Google Earth Engine (GEE) combining Sentinel-2 multispectral imagery, SRTM DEMs, and Random Forest machine learning across 2,700 acres to automate burn scar perimeter extraction, grade burn severity, and model post-fire soil stability risks.
      </p>

      <!-- Action Buttons -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 mb-8">
        <a 
          href="https://youtu.be/jvp7yW0xpqY?si=0dyH4guKxP4TKxUg" 
          target="_blank" 
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center bg-red-700 hover:bg-red-600 text-white font-semibold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg transition-colors shadow-md"
        >
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 mr-2 fill-current" viewBox="0 0 24 24">
            <path d="M23.498 6.186a3.016 3.016 0 0 0-2.122-2.136C19.505 3.545 12 3.545 12 3.545s-7.505 0-9.377.505A3.017 3.017 0 0 0 .502 6.186C0 8.07 0 12 0 12s0 3.93.502 5.814a3.016 3.016 0 0 0 2.122 2.136c1.871.505 9.376.505 9.376.505s7.505 0 9.377-.505a3.015 3.015 0 0 0 2.122-2.136C24 15.93 24 12 24 12s0-3.93-.502-5.814zM9.545 15.568V8.432L15.818 12l-6.273 3.568z"/>
          </svg>
          Watch YouTube Presentation
        </a>
        <a 
          href="https://github.com/arikwaldinger/rx_fire-intelligence-gee" 
          target="_blank" 
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-block bg-emerald-700 hover:bg-emerald-600 text-white font-semibold px-6 py-3 rounded-lg transition-colors shadow-md"
        >
          View GEE Repository
        </a>
      </div>

      <!-- Institutional & Data Disclaimer -->
      <div class="bg-gray-800/80 border-l-4 border-amber-500 p-4 rounded-r-lg mb-10 text-xs sm:text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-amber-400 font-semibold block mb-1">Disclaimer:</strong>
        This project and associated presentation were prepared independently and are not part of my official role with the Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources. Any opinions, findings, and conclusions expressed are my own and should not be interpreted as representing the Department. All data utilized is publicly available.
      </div>

  <!-- Main Visual Graphic & Legend -->
      <div class="my-8 bg-gray-800 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-700 shadow-lg">
        <img 
          src="/portfolio/Avoca_DNBR.png" 
          alt="Delta Normalized Burn Ratio (dNBR) Map of Avoca Prairie and Savanna Spring 2025 Burn" 
          class="w-full h-auto rounded-md border border-gray-700"
        />
        
        <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-gray-700">
          <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-200 text-center mb-3">
            Figure 1: Delta Normalized Burn Ratio (&Delta;NBR) evaluation of the Spring 2025 500-acre prescribed burn unit at Avoca Prairie and Savanna State Natural Area.
          </p>
          
          <!-- Map Key / Legend -->
          <div class="bg-gray-900/80 p-3 rounded border border-gray-700 text-xs sm:text-sm">
            <span class="block font-semibold text-emerald-400 mb-2 text-center uppercase tracking-wider">Burn Severity Map Legend (&Delta;NBR)</span>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 gap-3">
              <div class="flex items-center space-x-2">
                <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-[#a8d08d] inline-block shrink-0"></span>
                <span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Low Severity:</strong> 0.05 – 0.20</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center space-x-2">
                <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-[#ffd966] inline-block shrink-0"></span>
                <span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Mod-Low Severity:</strong> 0.20 – 0.35</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center space-x-2">
                <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-[#f4b942] inline-block shrink-0"></span>
                <span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">Mod-High Severity:</strong> 0.35 – 0.55</span>
              </div>
              <div class="flex items-center space-x-2">
                <span class="w-3 h-3 rounded-full bg-[#c00000] inline-block shrink-0"></span>
                <span class="text-gray-300"><strong class="text-white">High Severity:</strong> &ge; 0.55</span>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Section 1: Ecological Mandate -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-4 border-b border-gray-800 pb-2">
          1. Ecological Mandate & Problem Statement
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Historically, Southern Wisconsin’s tallgrass prairies and oak savannas were maintained by low-intensity surface fires that recycled nutrients and suppressed shade-tolerant brush. Decades of fire suppression have led to heavy woody encroachment and choked understories. While prescribed fire is critical for ecological restoration, traditional success metrics rely on simple "blackened acres" rather than structural outcomes or soil health.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
          In intense burn units or on steep Driftless Area slopes, overburning can bake soils into a hydrophobic state, destroying root structures and triggering severe post-fire erosion and watershed sedimentation. This project engineered an automated cloud pipeline in Google Earth Engine (GEE) to scientifically evaluate burn severity, quantify structural effectiveness, and identify soil stability risks across a 2,700-acre portfolio.
        </p>

        <!-- Quick Metrics Banner -->
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 sm:grid-cols-4 gap-4 bg-gray-800 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-700 text-center text-sm my-6">
          <div>
            <span class="block text-gray-400 text-xs uppercase">Portfolio Extent</span>
            <strong class="text-white text-base">2,700 Acres</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block text-gray-400 text-xs uppercase">Random Forest Acc.</span>
            <strong class="text-emerald-400 text-base">94.4%</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block text-gray-400 text-xs uppercase">Jaccard Index (IoU)</span>
            <strong class="text-white text-base">0.93</strong>
          </div>
          <div>
            <span class="block text-gray-400 text-xs uppercase">Feature Stack</span>
            <strong class="text-emerald-400 text-base">33 Bands</strong>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 2: Data Pipeline & 33-Band Stack -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-4 border-b border-gray-800 pb-2">
          2. Multi-Sensor Data Fusion & 33-Band Architecture
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
          Capturing fleeting ash signatures across heterogeneous Midwestern cover types requires going beyond standard post-fire imagery. The model builds a 33-band feature stack per pixel across three seasonal time steps (Pre-Fire, Mid-Season Phenology, and Post-Fire):
        </p>

        <!-- Band Glossary Summary Table -->
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">Band Feature Glossary</h3>
        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-gray-300 border border-gray-700 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-gray-800 text-emerald-400 font-semibold uppercase text-xs">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Band Family / Index</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Spectral Components</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Ecological Mechanism</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-800 bg-gray-900/50">
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">SWIR Bands (B11, B12)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">1610nm & 2190nm</td>
                <td class="p-3">High sensitivity to surface charcoal deposition, ash retention, and soil moisture loss.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">BAIS2</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">Red-Edge & SWIR2</td>
                <td class="p-3">Burn Area Index for Sentinel-2, specifically tuned to isolate charcoal signatures post-burn.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">MIRBI</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">SWIR2 (B12) & SWIR1 (B11)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Mid-Infrared Burn Index, optimal for detecting charred woody debris in forest understories.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">NBR & NBR2</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">NIR (B8) & SWIR2 (B12)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Normalized Burn Ratio; industry standard for quantifying canopy and ground severity.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">NDVI & NDRE</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">NIR (B8), Red (B4), Red-Edge (B5)</td>
                <td class="p-3">Measures live green vegetation vigor and pre/post canopy leaf area density.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Pre / Mid / Delta Prefixes</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">Temporal Comparisons</td>
                <td class="p-3">Evaluates baseline unburned state, mid-spring phenological greenup, and delta change ($\Delta$).</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <!-- Ranked Variable Importance Breakdown -->
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-3">33-Band Ranked Variable Importance (Gini Score)</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-300 text-sm mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Random Forest variable importance analysis revealed that <strong>mid-season SWIR indices dominated model decisions</strong>. Because black ash is a fleeting signal quickly erased by spring rains, mid-season SWIR and phenology bands capture the persistent spectral signature of combustion residue:
        </p>
        <div class="bg-gray-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-gray-800 font-mono text-xs text-gray-300 overflow-x-auto mb-6">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-3 gap-2">
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">01. midBAI:</span> 224.19</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">02. midBAIS2:</span> 204.04</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">03. MIRBI:</span> 169.54</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">04. midNDVI:</span> 168.51</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">05. dMIRBI:</span> 164.35</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">06. midNBR:</span> 159.06</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">07. midBSI:</span> 157.35</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">08. MNDWI:</span> 155.20</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">09. NBR_NDVI:</span> 142.22</div>
            <div><span class="text-emerald-400">10. dNDVI:</span> 141.99</div>
            <div><span>11. preNDVI:</span> 141.04</div>
            <div><span>12. midNDRE:</span> 139.95</div>
            <div><span>13. SWIR_ratio:</span> 139.56</div>
            <div><span>14. preBSI:</span> 132.17</div>
            <div><span>15. BAIS2:</span> 129.15</div>
            <div><span>16. NBR2:</span> 128.91</div>
            <div><span>17. NDVI:</span> 128.36</div>
            <div><span>18. preNBR:</span> 126.45</div>
            <div><span>19. preCSI:</span> 125.79</div>
            <div><span>20. dBAI:</span> 123.81</div>
            <div><span>21. preBAI:</span> 122.97</div>
            <div><span>22. BAI:</span> 122.59</div>
            <div><span>23. dBAIS2:</span> 122.49</div>
            <div><span>24. EVI:</span> 122.37</div>
            <div><span>25. dNBR:</span> 122.22</div>
            <div><span>26. NDRE:</span> 117.39</div>
            <div><span>27. dBSI:</span> 112.30</div>
            <div><span>28. preNDRE:</span> 109.11</div>
            <div><span>29. NBR:</span> 102.02</div>
            <div><span>30. BSI:</span> 99.99</div>
            <div><span>31. CRI:</span> 96.75</div>
            <div><span>32. dNDRE:</span> 95.66</div>
            <div><span>33. CSI:</span> 83.86</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 3: Calibrated Prescribed Fire Severity Thresholds -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-4 border-b border-gray-800 pb-2">
          3. Calibrated Burn Severity Thresholds (USGS vs. Prescribed Fire)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          Standard USGS burn severity thresholds are calibrated for stand-replacing wildfires. Because intentional prescribed burns are managed at lower intensities, applying raw wildfire thresholds underestimates structural treatment efficacy.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
          To account for this, the pipeline <strong>shifts classification thresholds down by 0.05–0.10</strong>. For example, dNBR values peaking between <strong>0.35 and 0.57</strong> (documented at Natural Bridge and Lower Narrows) represent ideal moderate-high severity understory brush knockdowns in prescribed fire management:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-gray-300 border border-gray-700 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-gray-800 text-emerald-400 font-semibold uppercase text-xs">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Severity Class</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Standard USGS dNBR Range</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Prescribed Fire Calibrated dNBR</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Ecological Interpretation</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-800 bg-gray-900/50">
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Unburned / Regrowth</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono">&lt; 0.10</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">&lt; 0.05</td>
                <td class="p-3">Unburned refugia or rapid herbaceous regrowth.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Low Severity</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.10 – 0.27</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">0.05 – 0.20</td>
                <td class="p-3">Light litter scorching; canopy unaffected.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">Moderate-Low Severity</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono">0.27 – 0.44</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300">0.20 – 0.35</td>
                <td class="p-3">Effective surface fuel consumption & sapling top-kill.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="bg-emerald-950/30">
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-300">Moderate-High Severity</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300 font-bold">0.44 – 0.66</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300 font-bold">0.35 – 0.55</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Target understory brush suppression in savannas/woodlands.</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-white">High Severity</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono">&gt; 0.66</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-red-400 font-bold">&gt; 0.55</td>
                <td class="p-3">Heavy canopy scorch or potential overburn risk.</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 4: Multi-Unit Portfolio Analysis Table -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-4 border-b border-gray-800 pb-2">
          4. Statewide Prescribed Burn Portfolio Summary
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
          Mean &Delta;NBR response and timing windows across the 15 monitored burn units in Southern Wisconsin:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-gray-300 border border-gray-700 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-gray-800 text-emerald-400 font-semibold uppercase text-xs">
              <tr>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Burn Unit Name</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Mean dNBR</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Primary Ecological Landcover</th>
                <th class="p-3 border-b border-gray-700">Burn Window</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-800 bg-gray-900/50">
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Avoca Prairie & Savanna</td><td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300 font-bold">0.315</td><td class="p-3">Wetland / Lowland Prairie</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-900/50 text-blue-300 text-xs">Early Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Cruson Smith Slough</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.100</td><td class="p-3">Woodland / Wetland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-900/50 text-blue-300 text-xs">Early Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Arena Pines & Barrens</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.162</td><td class="p-3">Woodland / Wetland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-900/50 text-blue-300 text-xs">Early Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Mazomanie Barrens</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.215</td><td class="p-3">Barrens / Oak Savanna</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-blue-900/50 text-blue-300 text-xs">Early Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Marbleseed Prairie</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.233</td><td class="p-3">Dry Prairie / Grassland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-900/50 text-amber-300 text-xs">Mid Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Blue River Sands</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.189</td><td class="p-3">Sand Barrens / Open Dunes</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-900/50 text-amber-300 text-xs">Mid Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Devil's Lake (South Bluff)</td><td class="p-3 font-mono text-emerald-300 font-bold">0.371</td><td class="p-3">Upland Oak Woodland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-purple-900/50 text-purple-300 text-xs">Late Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Dells of Wisconsin River</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.223</td><td class="p-3">Gorge Woodland / Pine Relict</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-purple-900/50 text-purple-300 text-xs">Late Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr class="bg-emerald-950/20"><td class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-300">Hardscrabble Prairie</td><td class="p-3 font-mono font-bold text-emerald-300">0.539</td><td class="p-3">Woodland / Savanna / Grassland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-purple-900/50 text-purple-300 text-xs">Late Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Yellowstone Wildlife Area</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.295</td><td class="p-3">Woodland / Savanna / Grassland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-purple-900/50 text-purple-300 text-xs">Late Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">York Prairie</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.223</td><td class="p-3">Remnant Grassland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-900/50 text-amber-300 text-xs">Mid Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Bakkens Pond</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.140</td><td class="p-3">Oak Savanna / Grassland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-900/50 text-amber-300 text-xs">Mid Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Belmont Prairie</td><td class="p-3 font-mono text-gray-400">-0.130</td><td class="p-3">Wet-Mesic Grassland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-amber-900/50 text-amber-300 text-xs">Mid Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Lower Narrows</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.086</td><td class="p-3">Talus Slope Woodland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-purple-900/50 text-purple-300 text-xs">Late Spring</span></td></tr>
              <tr><td class="p-3 font-medium text-white">Natural Bridge State Park</td><td class="p-3 font-mono">0.095</td><td class="p-3">Sandstone Cliff Woodland</td><td class="p-3"><span class="px-2 py-0.5 rounded bg-purple-900/50 text-purple-300 text-xs">Late Spring</span></td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- Section 5: Long-Term Vegetation Recovery (NDVI) -->
      <section class="mb-12">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-400 mb-4 border-b border-gray-800 pb-2">
          5. Multi-Year Vegetation Recovery & Summer Greenness (NDVI)
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
          To verify that prescribed fire stimulated native vigor rather than long-term degradation, peak summer greenness (July–August NDVI) was computed across historical baselines (2022–2024 average) and compared against post-fire Summer 2025 performance.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-300 mb-6 leading-relaxed">
          <strong>Key Result:</strong> Open grassland and prairie units experienced significant post-burn vigor surges (e.g., <strong>+9.1% at York Prairie</strong> and <strong>+4.3% at Belmont Prairie</strong>) due to dark soil warming and thatch removal. Dense timber units like Dells of Wisconsin saw temporary canopy reductions (-3.1%), indicating desired understory thinning:
        </p>

        <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
          <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-gray-300 border border-gray-700 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
            <thead class="bg-gray-800 text-emerald-400 font-semibold uppercase text-xs">
              <tr>
